CLAIM|id=C1|status=SUPPORTED_NAMED|why=Measured: der(O) dim 14 (rank 50 of 64, two tables), stab(e7) dim 8 with commutant span(I,J) on T, m dim 6, orbit map rank 6. Group level (transitivity, stabiliser is SU(3) not just su(3)) uses G2 connected and S^6 simply connected: NAMED, Harvey 1990 Spinors and Calibrations ch.6; Baez 2002 Bull AMS 39 sec 4.1; Bryant 1987 Ann Math 126.|restate=none|claim=NAMED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C2|status=SUPPORTED_MEASURED|why=J^2:-1, orthogonal, commutes with su(3); commutant span(I,J) and zero antilinear intertwiners, so (T,J) is an irreducible complex 3-dim rep of complex type. Whether it is called 3 or 3bar is a choice of isomorphism h:su(3): with (T,J) as defining rep it is the 3; M2 labelling calls +J the 3bar.|restate=T_xS^6 with J_x is an irreducible complex 3-dimensional SU(3) representation of complex type; (T,J) and (T,-J) are the 3 and 3bar in some order fixed by convention.|claim=MEASURED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C3|status=SUPPORTED_MEASURED|why=Nonintegrability measured two independent ways: M1 Lie-algebraic N plus Koszul (h_C+m10 not closed, [m10,m10] in m01 by eps), and this run's ambient vector-field Nijenhuis on S^6 with no Lie algebra (24 of 36 ordered pairs nonzero, N:-4J(nabla J), equal for +J and -J, both octonion tables, two points), matching M1's sample exactly (4x geometric). Control S2xS2xS2 gives N:0. CP^2 clause: the integrable reason is that m10 is an abelian subalgebra ([m10,m10]:0, g has a Z-grading g_-1+g_0+g_1), while G2/SU(3) has a Z3-grading with [g_1,g_1] in g_2:3bar. Hopf problem open: NAMED, Agricola-Bazzoni-Goertsches-Konstantis-Rollenske 2018 Diff Geom Appl 57; no ROUND-metric-orthogonal integrable J: LeBrun 1987 Proc AMS 101.|restate=Replace 'because the 2 there is abelian' with 'because m^(1,0):C^2 is an abelian subalgebra, so [m10,m10]:0 lies in h_C+m10'.|claim=MEASURED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C4|status=SUPPORTED_NAMED|why=Measured pointwise on S^6 (two tables, two points): (nabla_X J)Y:pi_T(X x Y) antisymmetric so (nabla_X J)X:0, and nabla J nonzero, i.e. strict nearly Kahler, using the Gauss-formula connection (NAMED). Cone statement NAMED: Gray 1976 Math Ann 223; Fukami-Ishihara 1955 Tohoku Math J 7; Bar 1993 Comm Math Phys 154 (nearly Kahler 6-manifolds iff cone holonomy in G2).|restate=none|claim=NAMED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C5|status=SUPPORTED_MEASURED|why=Measured here and in M2: ;W;:12 with order histogram 1:1,2:7,3:2,6:2 (D6:S3xZ2), W(A2) from long roots order 6 without -I, W:W(A2)x{+-I}, short roots two W(A2)-orbits of 3 swapped by -I; diag(1,1,-1,1,-1,-1,-1) is an automorphism with det +1, sends e7 to -e7, conjugates J to -J and acts as -1 on the Cartan.|restate=none|claim=MEASURED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C6|status=SUPPORTED_MEASURED|why=Tree-level algebra measured: stabiliser dim 8, 3<Xv,Yv>:-tr(XY) on the 6-dim m so the six broken masses are equal, Casimir -2 on R^7. Vacuum manifold S^6 relies on C1 (NAMED). Mass formula NAMED: Weinberg QFT vol II sec 21.1. Degeneracy is forced by Schur (one invariant symmetric form on an irreducible complex-type m); J supplies the complex structure that makes m the complex 3.|restate=Scope: classical tree-level Higgs mechanism; no statement about the quantum theory.|claim=MEASURED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C7|status=SUPPORTED_MEASURED|why=Measured in both tables: T:*(phi^.) has eigenvalues 2 (dim 7) and -1 (dim 14) when phi is positive for the orientation, der(O) is the -1 eigenspace, and on all 7 coassociative planes the ASD half (C oriented by psi) lies in Lambda2_14 with F^psi:0. Signs 2/-1 and the label ASD flip with orientation conventions; 'one chirality' is the convention-free statement. G2-instanton equivalence NAMED: Donaldson-Thomas 1998 (Gauge theory in higher dimensions, Geometric Universe, OUP); Corrigan-Devchand-Fairlie-Nuyts 1983 Nucl Phys B 214. Pulled-back BPST has infinite action on R^7.|restate=none|claim=MEASURED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C8|status=OVERSTATED|why=The Jordan-limit argument (M3 LIMIT steps 1-8) is sound: Hausdorff/Chabauty limit H of finite subgroups has an abelian closed subgroup of finite index, so H0 is a torus of dim <: N-1. 'At most T x; W' does not follow as containment: F_m:Z_m*2I in S(U(2)xU(1)) in SU(3) converges to H:U(1)*2I, with H/H0 of order 60 and every abelian normal subgroup inside H0, so H lies in no conjugate of N(T) (whose abelian normal T has index 6). Delta(6n^2):(Z_n^2)x;S3 in N(T) measured n:1..7.|restate=Every Hausdorff limit of finite subgroups of SU(N) is torus-by-finite with identity component a torus of dimension at most N-1; N(T):T x; W attains that dimension (limit of Delta(6n^2)); limits need not lie in N(T) (example U(1)*2I in SU(3)); so no tower of finite subgroups approaches SU(N).|claim=NAMED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C9|status=OVERSTATED|why=S3 in the finite repo is Delta(6):{sgn(s)P_s} in N(T), which maps onto W(SU(3)) and meets T trivially (measured diag_order 1). Maximal-abelian projection ('t Hooft 1981 Nucl Phys B 190; Kronfeld-Schierholz-Wiese 1987 Nucl Phys B 293) keeps the torus U(1)^2 and leaves W as residual symmetry; it does not leave S3. Per the repo README (worktree head 6ec2a808, scoped, not the owning gate) the certified gap is E9-E8 above the 8 flat S3 states on 2D spatial tori for r:J_B/J_E>:9, the D(S3) quantum-double phase; this run measures 8:;Hom(Z^2,S3)/S3;:number of D(S3) irreps, and 21 for Delta(24):S4, growing with n (84 for Delta(54), 103 for Delta(96)). The README plaquette weight 2-chi(U_p) uses the 2-dim irrep (the W(A2) reflection character on the Cartan, measured), whereas Re tr_3 of SU(3) restricted to Delta(6) is sign+std (measured), so the model is an S3 gauge theory with its own action, not the SU(3) Wilson action restricted to a subgroup. A finite positive transfer kernel is gapped for every positive weight (toy measured 1000/1000; Perron-Frobenius NAMED). Geometry of G2/SU(3) supplies no gap.|restate=The S3 lattice theory has gauge group Delta(6):S3 in N(T) in SU(3), isomorphic to W(SU(3)) in W(G2); its certified gaps are statements about finite 2D spatial tori in the D(S3) topological phase above 8 flat sectors; transfer to continuum SU(N) in 3+1 dimensions needs a proof that is not yet available and is OPEN.|claim=NAMED|json=0
CLAIM|id=C10|status=OVERSTATED|why=Routes are real and NAMED: Wilson 1974 PRD 10 2445; Kogut-Susskind 1975 PRD 11 395; q-deformed SU(2)_k: Zache-Gonzalez-Cuadra-Zoller 2023 PRL 131 171902; non-subgroup partitions: Hartung-Jakobs-Jansen-Ostmeyer-Urbach 2022 EPJC 82 237; quantum links: Chandrasekharan-Wiese 1997 Nucl Phys B 492 455. The Clay statement (Jaffe-Witten 2000, CMI) is on R^4: existence for every compact simple G with OS/Wightman axioms and a gap Delta>0 of the infinite-volume Hamiltonian. A gap at fixed physical size is one step; the gap must also stay bounded below as volume goes to infinity, with nontriviality and restored O(4). Closest rigorous fixed-volume continuum results: Balaban 1987-89 CMP (UV stability, 4D lattice YM on a finite torus); Magnen-Rivasseau-Seneor 1993 CMP 155 (YM4 with an infrared cutoff).|restate=Clay asks for YM on R^4 for each compact simple G, satisfying OS/Wightman axioms incl. Euclidean invariance and nontriviality, with an infinite-volume mass gap. A lattice route needs continuum limit a->0 with asymptotic-freedom renormalisation, OS reconstruction, and a gap bounded below uniformly in both a->0 and L->infinity; the fixed-physical-size finite-universe target is a separate, well-posed statement whose transfer to R^4 is OPEN.|claim=NAMED|json=0
